<DocsLayout title="Deployment" eyebrow="Operational topology" intro="One public RustyAuth service and one private, persistent SableDB service.">
  <div class="deployment-grid"><div><small>Public edge</small><strong>RustyAuth</strong><p>TLS terminated · health checks · one writer in 0.1</p></div><div><small>Private network</small><strong>SableDB</strong><p>No public endpoint · persistent volume · internal port 6379</p></div><div><small>Downstream</small><strong>Your app</strong><p>Verify issuer · audience · tenant · expiry</p></div></div>
  <h2>One-click Railway deployment</h2><p>The Railway template creates both services inside the <strong>RustyAuth</strong> template group, generates the master key and bootstrap token, links SableDB over the private network and attaches its persistent volume. The gRPC-enabled image also requires distinct <code>AUTH_EVENT_RPC_TOKEN</code> and <code>AUTH_IDENTITY_RPC_TOKEN</code> values of at least 32 characters. You supply the browser application's exact HTTPS origin and matching WebAuthn RP ID during the environment step.</p><p><a class="button button-railway" href="https://railway.com/new/template/rustyauth?utm_medium=integration&utm_source=button&utm_campaign=rustyauth">Deploy on Railway <span>↗</span></a></p><p>RustyAuth remains pre-release software. Use the template for evaluation and integration work, not as the sole identity system for a production service.</p>
  <h2>Container deployment</h2><p>The supplied container builds the Rust service as a non-root user. Deploy the SableDB container separately on the same private network and attach a durable volume at its documented data path. Native gRPC requires HTTP/2 through the public proxy; disable buffering and preserve RPC trailers for the subscription route.</p>
  <h2>Scaling</h2><p>The authentication service is lightweight and can scale vertically with predictable resource use. Version 0.1 intentionally supports one writer replica while multi-process coordination and operational recovery are completed.</p>
  <h2>Backup before scale</h2><p>Do not treat replication as backup. Capture the SableDB volume, restore into a clean environment, and verify credentials, counters, sessions and signing state before calling the system production-ready.</p>
</DocsLayout>
